Inspect identity|id|Prints the current user's UID, primary group and supplementary groups.|Check the credentials involved in a permission failure.|uid is the user identity; gid is the primary group; groups lists additional memberships.|uid=1000(app) gid=1000(app) groups=1000(app),1001(readers)|Read-only; run in the same identity context as the failing process.
Inspect path permissions|namei -l /srv/app/config.yml|Walks a path and displays permissions and ownership for each component.|Locate a parent directory that blocks traversal despite readable file permissions.|Each row is a path component; directory execute permission is required for traversal.|drwx------ root root srv|Read-only; replace the example path. namei is commonly supplied by util-linux.
Disk capacity|df -hT|Reports mounted-filesystem block capacity with readable units and filesystem type.|Identify the full filesystem before cleaning or expanding storage.|Use% is block utilization; Mounted on identifies the mount, not just a directory name.|Filesystem Type Size Used Avail Use% Mounted on|Read-only; compare the mount containing the failing path.
Inode capacity|df -i|Reports inode allocation for mounted filesystems.|Detect no-space failures caused by many small files.|IUse% tracks inode use; free bytes can remain when IFree reaches zero.|Filesystem Inodes IUsed IFree IUse% Mounted on|Read-only; inode semantics vary by filesystem.
Memory headroom|free -h|Summarizes memory and swap accounting.|Distinguish reclaimable cache from genuine memory shortage.|Available is estimated usable headroom; free alone excludes useful reclaimable cache.|Mem: total used free shared buff/cache available|Read-only; correlate with pressure and swap activity.
Process states|ps -eo pid,ppid,user,stat,etime,comm|Prints selected fields for every visible process.|Find parent-child relationships, zombies and blocked tasks.|STAT includes R for runnable, S for interruptible sleep, D for uninterruptible sleep and Z for zombie; ETIME is elapsed lifetime.|PID PPID USER STAT ELAPSED COMMAND|Read-only; visibility depends on namespace and permissions.
Listening sockets|ss -lntp|Lists listening TCP sockets numerically and requests process information.|Check whether a service listens on the expected address and port.|-l selects listeners, -n avoids name lookup, -t selects TCP, -p adds process data when permitted.|LISTEN 0 128 127.0.0.1:8080 0.0.0.0:*|Read-only; loopback binding is not external reachability.
System activity|vmstat 1 5|Samples process, memory, paging, I/O and CPU counters five times.|Separate CPU contention, swapping and I/O waits.|The first row summarizes since boot; later rows cover intervals. r is runnable tasks, b is blocked tasks, si/so are swap traffic.|r b swpd free buff cache si so bi bo in cs us sy id wa st|Read-only; interpret several signals together rather than one column.
Kernel events|journalctl -k --since '-15 min' --no-pager|Shows recent kernel journal messages.|Find OOM, device and filesystem evidence near an incident.|-k selects kernel messages; --since bounds time. Match timestamps and affected devices or cgroups.|kernel: ... Out of memory ...|Read-only; access and retention vary, and logs may contain sensitive metadata.
Mount inspection|findmnt -o TARGET,SOURCE,FSTYPE,OPTIONS|Displays mounted filesystems and selected fields.|Find read-only mounts or unexpected backing devices.|TARGET is the path; SOURCE is the backing object; ro in OPTIONS means read-only.|/data /dev/mapper/vg-data ext4 ro,relatime|Read-only; do not remount until the reason for read-only state is understood.
Open deleted files|lsof +L1|Lists open files with link counts below one.|Explain disk space retained after a log was deleted.|Look at COMMAND, PID, SIZE/OFF and NAME marked deleted; the process still holds the inode.|app 123 app 4w REG ... /var/log/app.log (deleted)|Read-only but may require elevated visibility; do not kill a process solely from this output.
System resolver|getent hosts example.com|Queries the configured name-service lookup path.|Compare application-style resolution with direct DNS tools.|Returned addresses follow system NSS behavior and can include local overrides.|192.0.2.10 example.com|Read-only; example addresses are illustrative and actual results differ.
List metadata|ls -lah /srv/app|Lists directory entries with mode, owner, size and modification time.|Compare visible file attributes during access or deployment checks.|The first character is file type; the next nine are permission bits. -a includes dotfiles; sizes are readable units.|-rw-r----- 1 app readers 4.0K Oct 23 10:00 config.yml|Read-only; listing a directory requires access to it and does not prove every file is readable.
Precise file metadata|stat /srv/app/config.yml|Reports inode metadata and timestamps for a filesystem object.|Check ownership, permissions, link count and actual allocated storage.|Size is logical length; Blocks reports allocated blocks. Access, Modify and Change are different timestamps.|Size: 4096 Blocks: 8 Links: 1|Read-only; use the intended path and distinguish metadata change from content modification.
Directory usage|du -xhd1 /var|Totals allocated space beneath a directory one level deep.|Find which directory contributes to a full filesystem.|-x stays on one filesystem; -d1 limits display depth. Totals can differ from df for open deleted files and filesystem metadata.|1.2G /var/log|Read-only but can generate substantial I/O; permissions can prevent a complete scan. GNU du syntax.
Find old files|find /var/log/app -xdev -type f -mtime +7 -print|Finds regular files whose modification age exceeds the specified day boundary.|Inventory retention candidates before deciding what to remove.|-xdev avoids other mounted filesystems; -mtime counts rounded 24-hour periods. Output is matching paths, not deletion confirmation.|/var/log/app/worker.log.9|Read-only example; inspect results and retention requirements before adding any deleting action.
Search log context|grep -n -C 2 -- 'ERROR' /var/log/app.log|Finds matching lines and nearby context in a text file.|Locate an error with enough surrounding information to interpret it.|-n prints line numbers; -C 2 adds two lines on each side. -- ends option parsing.|42:ERROR connection refused|Read-only; logs may contain sensitive fields. A missing match can mean wrong time window or file.
Follow rotating logs|tail -F /var/log/app.log|Displays the end of a file and follows its name across replacement.|Observe new messages from an application that rotates log files.|-F follows by name and retries if the path disappears; new lines are live output, not a complete incident history.|2026-10-23T10:00:00Z worker ready|Read-only; stop with Ctrl-C and protect sensitive log output.
Bounded file inspection|head -n 20 /etc/services|Prints the first twenty lines of a file.|Inspect a file format without dumping the entire file to the terminal.|-n sets the line count; output preserves the selected lines, including comments.|# Network services|Read-only; do not use indiscriminately on secret files.
Interactive file reading|less /var/log/app.log|Opens a pager for navigating text without editing it.|Read a large log and search for a known incident marker.|Use /pattern to search, n for the next match and q to quit; position is within the opened file.|Pager showing log lines|Read-only viewing; the pager may offer shell features, so it is not a security sandbox.
ACL inspection|getfacl /srv/app/config.yml|Displays POSIX access-control entries and their effective permissions.|Diagnose access that cannot be explained by mode bits alone.|Named-user and group-class entries can be limited by mask; an effective comment shows the resulting rights.|user:analyst:rw- #effective:r--|Read-only; ACL tooling and filesystem support must be installed.
Scoped permission change|chmod u=rw,g=r,o= /srv/app/config.yml|Sets explicit owner, group and other permission bits on one file.|Apply a reviewed file-access policy without recursive broad changes.|No output normally means success; inspect with stat afterward. This example sets mode 640.|No output on success; stat shows 640|Mutates file permissions; verify file identity, intended owner/group and parent traversal first.
Scoped ownership change|chown app:readers /srv/app/config.yml|Changes the owner and group of a selected filesystem object.|Align a file with an approved service identity and access group.|app is the owner and readers is the group; success is normally silent. Verify with stat or ls.|No output on success|Requires authority to change ownership; affects access and can clear special mode bits. Avoid recursive use without review.
Resolve a link|readlink -f /srv/app/current|Resolves links and prints a canonicalized path.|Find the release directory selected by a deployment link.|The output is the resolved path, not the contents of the target; missing path components can cause failure.|/srv/app/releases/2026-10-23|Read-only; -f behavior is GNU/coreutils-style and does not prove the application has reloaded the target.
Process lookup|pgrep -af 'worker'|Lists processes whose full command line matches an expression.|Find candidate processes before inspecting identity or sending signals.|Each row begins with a PID followed by its command line; a substring can match unrelated processes.|2481 /usr/bin/worker --config /etc/app.yml|Read-only; verify the result and process start time. Arguments may contain sensitive data.
Graceful termination|kill -TERM PID|Sends a termination request to one process.|Allow a process to perform its supported shutdown handling.|Replace PID with a verified current process ID. A successful send does not prove cleanup or exit has completed.|No output on successful signal delivery|Changes process state; confirm identity, ownership and service-manager restart policy before use.
Live process view|top -p PID|Shows repeated resource and state information for a selected process.|Observe whether demand or state changes while reproducing a problem.|%CPU describes CPU use over sampling intervals; RES is resident memory. Press q to exit.|PID USER PR NI VIRT RES SHR S %CPU %MEM TIME+ COMMAND|Read-only inspection; some interactive keys can change priorities or send signals, so use deliberately.
Per-process activity|pidstat -p PID 1 5|Samples CPU-related activity for one process five times.|Correlate a specific process with an incident instead of relying on host averages.|Each interval reports the selected PID; %CPU and CPU indicate use and execution CPU. PID may exit during sampling.|UID PID %usr %system %guest %wait %CPU CPU Command|Read-only; requires sysstat. Replace PID and compare with workload timing.
Storage latency|iostat -xz 1 5|Reports extended device I/O statistics at one-second intervals.|Investigate storage delay and queueing during a slowdown.|await is average I/O completion time; aqu-sz is average queue length. %util alone is not a universal saturation limit for parallel devices.|Device r/s w/s r_await w_await aqu-sz %util|Read-only; requires sysstat. The first report covers since boot unless configured otherwise.
Pressure stalls|cat /proc/pressure/memory|Reads Linux pressure-stall information for memory.|Check whether memory contention is delaying runnable work.|some measures intervals with at least one task stalled; full measures all non-idle tasks stalled. avg10/60/300 are percentage windows.|some avg10=2.00 avg60=0.80 avg300=0.20 total=123456|Read-only; requires a kernel exposing PSI. Host and cgroup scope are different.
Process descriptor limits|cat /proc/PID/limits|Displays the running process's resource limits.|Check the effective open-file limit rather than assuming the interactive shell's limit applies.|Max open files has soft and hard limits; the soft limit is the active boundary for normal allocation.|Max open files 1024 4096 files|Read-only; replace PID. Visibility depends on procfs permissions and process lifetime.
Process file descriptors|ls -l /proc/PID/fd|Lists a process's open descriptor links.|Inspect whether sockets, files or pipes dominate descriptor usage.|Each filename is a descriptor number; the link target identifies the opened object, sometimes marked deleted.|7 -> socket:[12345]|Read-only; access may be restricted. The process can open or close descriptors while you inspect it.
Trace selected calls|strace -f -e trace=file -p PID|Attaches a syscall tracer and follows child processes for file-related calls.|Find pathname failures such as ENOENT or EACCES when logs are insufficient.|A call's return value and errno show the kernel result; unfinished calls may be blocked rather than failed.|openat(..., "/etc/app.yml", O_RDONLY) = -1 EACCES|Diagnostic attachment can slow the target and reveal paths; obtain incident authority and keep the capture brief.
User and group lookup|getent passwd app|Queries the system account database for an account.|Check the UID, primary GID, home and shell associated with a service name.|Colon-separated fields include name, password placeholder, UID, GID, description, home and shell.|app:x:1001:1001:Application:/srv/app:/usr/sbin/nologin|Read-only; results may come from local files or a configured directory service.
File capabilities|getcap /usr/local/bin/app|Displays file-based Linux capabilities attached to an executable.|Review privileges granted outside normal owner/group mode bits.|The capability list and flags describe enabled file capabilities; empty output can mean none are set.|/usr/local/bin/app cap_net_bind_service=ep|Read-only; requires capability tools. Review the complete privilege model, not this one output alone.
Archive inventory|tar -tf backup.tar|Lists members of an archive without extracting them.|Inspect backup contents and path names before a restore.|-t lists and -f selects the archive file. Output shows member names, not a verified successful application restore.|srv/app/config.yml|Read-only; review paths and trusted origin before extraction, especially with elevated privileges.
Checksum verification|sha256sum artifact.tar|Computes a SHA-256 digest of a file.|Compare an artifact with an independently trusted expected digest.|The first field is the hex digest and the second is the filename; equal digests support byte identity, not publisher trust by themselves.|HEX_DIGEST  artifact.tar|Read-only; obtain the expected checksum from an authenticated source.
Structured JSON inspection|jq '.status' response.json|Selects a field from a JSON document.|Read a structured API result without unreliable text slicing.|Objects and arrays remain structured; null can mean the selected field is absent. Invalid JSON causes an error.|{"phase":"Ready"}|Read-only; jq must be installed. Avoid printing credential fields into shared logs.
Line and byte counts|wc -l -c /var/log/app.log|Counts newline characters and bytes in a file.|Measure a text file's size and line growth during investigation.|The first numeric column is line count and the second is bytes; a final unterminated line affects interpretation.|2500 180000 /var/log/app.log|Read-only; reading a large active file adds I/O and is only a point-in-time observation.
Command discovery|command -v python3|Reports how the current shell resolves a command name.|Compare interactive and scheduled-job PATH behavior.|A path identifies the executable found; no output with failure status means no matching command was resolved.|/usr/bin/python3|Read-only shell builtin; run in the same environment as the failing job.
